The Einstein relation for the photo‑electric process is  

\[
K_{\max}= \frac{hc}{\lambda}-\phi ,
\]

where \(K_{\max}\) is the measured kinetic energy, \(\lambda\) the wavelength of the incident light, \(h\) Planck’s constant, \(c\) the speed of light and \(\phi\) the work function.

**(a) Work function**

\[
\phi = \frac{hc}{\lambda}-K_{\max}
\]

\[
\frac{hc}{\lambda}= \frac{(6.63\times10^{-34}\ \text{J·s})(3.00\times10^{8}\ \text{m/s})}{4.1\times10^{-7}\ \text{m}}
                     \approx 4.85\times10^{-19}\ \text{J}
\]

\[
\phi = 4.85\times10^{-19}\ \text{J} - 3.20\times10^{-19}\ \text{J}
      \approx 1.6\times10^{-19}\ \text{J}
\]

**(b) Longest wavelength that can cause emission**

For the threshold case the kinetic energy is zero, so  

\[
\phi = \frac{hc}{\lambda_{\text{max}}}\;\; \Longrightarrow\;\;
\lambda_{\text{max}} = \frac{hc}{\phi}
\]

\[
\lambda_{\text{max}} = \frac{1.99\times10^{-25}\ \text{J·m}}{1.6\times10^{-19}\ \text{J}}
                     \approx 1.25\times10^{-6}\ \text{m}
\]

Thus the work function is about \(1.6\times10^{-19}\) J and the longest wavelength capable of ejecting electrons is about \(1.25\times10^{-6}\) m.

The pair of values that matches these results is option **C**.

**ANSWER: C**

